What is a Deadlock?
Official Definition

A deadlock occurs when a set of processes are permanently blocked because each process is waiting for a resource held by another process.

Easy Definition

Think:

I am waiting for you.

You are waiting for me.

Neither can move.

System gets stuck forever.

Real-Life Example 1: Two People

Imagine:

Rahul has:

Pen

and wants:

Notebook

Mohit has:

Notebook

and wants:

Pen

Situation:

Rahul waits for Mohit

Mohit waits for Rahul

Result:

Nobody proceeds

Deadlock.

Real-Life Example 2: Traffic Intersection

Four cars arrive.

Car A waits for Car B.

Car B waits for Car C.

Car C waits for Car D.

Car D waits for Car A.

Result:

Traffic Frozen

Deadlock.

OS Example

Suppose:

Process P1

Holds:

Printer

Needs:

Scanner
Process P2

Holds:

Scanner

Needs:

Printer

Result:

P1 waits for Scanner

P2 waits for Printer

Both wait forever.

Deadlock.

Visual Representation
P1 ---> Scanner

P2 ---> Printer

Printer ---> P1

Scanner ---> P2

Circular dependency.

Important Observation

Deadlock is NOT:

Slow System

Deadlock is:

No Progress Possible

The system may still be alive.

CPU may still run.

Other processes may work.

But affected processes are stuck forever.

Necessary Conditions for Deadlock

This is one of the most asked interview questions.

Deadlock can occur only if FOUR conditions exist simultaneously.

These are called:

Coffman Conditions

Named after Edward G. Coffman Jr..

Condition 1: Mutual Exclusion

Resource cannot be shared.

Only one process can use it.

Example:

Printer

Only one process prints at a time.

If everyone could share it simultaneously:

No deadlock.
Condition 2: Hold and Wait

Process already holds a resource and waits for another.

Example:

P1 holds:

Printer

and waits for:

Scanner

This is hold and wait.

Condition 3: No Preemption

Resource cannot be forcibly taken away.

Example:

Printer is allocated.

OS cannot simply snatch it away.

Process must voluntarily release it.

Condition 4: Circular Wait

Processes form a circle.

Example:

P1 waits for P2

P2 waits for P3

P3 waits for P4

P4 waits for P1

Circular chain.

Most Important Memory Trick

Remember:

M H N C

or

Mutual Exclusion
Hold and Wait
No Preemption
Circular Wait

If any one condition is removed:

Deadlock cannot occur.
Example Analysis

Suppose:

P1 has Printer
Needs Scanner

P2 has Scanner
Needs Printer

Check conditions.

Mutual Exclusion?

Yes

Printer shared?

No

Hold and Wait?

Yes

No Preemption?

Yes

Circular Wait?

Yes

All four present.

Result:

Deadlock
Deadlock vs Starvation

Students confuse these a lot.

Deadlock

Everybody waits.

Example:

P1 waits for P2

P2 waits for P1

Nobody progresses.

Starvation

One process waits forever.

Others continue.

Example:

High-priority processes keep running

Low-priority process never runs

System works.

But one process suffers.

Deadlock vs Livelock

Another interview favorite.

Deadlock

Processes do nothing.

Example:

Waiting forever
Livelock

Processes keep changing state.

But no useful work happens.

Example:

Two people in a corridor:

You move left.
I move left.

You move right.
I move right.

Both keep moving.

Nobody passes.

